 ## Overview
-Quadratic Taylor Inclusion Functions (QTIF) are a method for adapting step size in sphere tracing by using quadratic forward inclusion bounds instead of linear ones, explicitly taking the ray direction into account [S1]. They solve the problem of inefficient step sizes for grazing rays by providing tighter bounds when the function's derivative is Lipschitz continuous and computable on the fly [S1]. Unlike linear bounds, which only use the first derivative, QTIFs incorporate the second-order Lipschitz constant to bound the curvature of the function along the ray [S1]. Without enhancements, they are often slower than linear bounds due to the overhead of computing gradients and the exponential growth of the Lipschitz constant in fractal Brownian motion (FBM) [S1].
+Quadratic Taylor Inclusion Functions (QTIF) are a method for adapting step size in sphere tracing by using quadratic forward inclusion bounds instead of linear ones, explicitly taking the ray direction into account [S1]. They require the function derivative to be computable on the fly and Lipschitz continuous [S1]. Without enhancements, they often perform worse than linear bounds due to the overhead of computing gradients and the exponential growth of the second-order Lipschitz constant in fractal Brownian motion (FBM) [S1].
 
